---
/**
 * HeroStack — the homepage hero. V6, "isometric strata".
 *
 * APPROVED DESIGN, BUILT TO THE RENDER. Figma `wJ9DK6ByFUN6rWe0CpCVPU`, frame
 * `43:3` on page `Hero — FINAL (V6)`; the graphic alone is `39:2` on page
 * `30:2`. Owner decision, 2026-08-02, recorded in specs/HOMEPAGE-DECISION-TABLE.md
 * row 1. This file implements that render. It does not reinterpret it.
 *
 * WHAT IT REPLACES, AND WHY. The previous hero was Direction B, the receding
 * tender stack: four claim cards, three traced and one struck out, under the
 * eyebrow "Worked example · award question 4.2 · social value weighting 10%".
 * The headline was never the problem. That eyebrow was. It capped the company
 * at one UK procurement note in one financial year, and the site's narrative is
 * now market-neutral with UK public kept as the proof point rather than as the
 * whole story (owner decision, settles OA-25). The four-claim argument has not
 * been thrown away; it is the reasoning-trace section's entire subject, which
 * is where a detailed refusal belongs. A hero has to say what the product is.
 *
 * THE IDEA. Three planes of light, stacked in depth, read top to bottom:
 * Qualify, then Win, then Get paid. That is the headline directly above,
 * rendered as geometry, and it is the whole lifecycle claim in one picture.
 *
 * THE SEQUENCE RUNS DOWNWARD AND MUST NEVER BE FLIPPED. An earlier build ran
 * it upward, so the eye met "Get paid" first and the picture argued against the
 * tagline sitting six inches above it. `39:2` used to carry that older ordering
 * and was corrected on 2026-08-02, so both frames now agree with each other and
 * with this file. If you ever find yourself reordering STRATA below, you are
 * undoing a fix.
 *
 * DEPTH IS MADE OF GEOMETRY AND LIGHT, NEVER OF DEGRADED TEXT. The hero this
 * replaces carried depth with opacity down to 0.38 and blur up to 2.4px applied
 * to the elements holding the text, which is a WCAG 1.4.3 failure rather than a
 * style. Every word in this component renders at a full-contrast token colour
 * with no blur and no opacity below 1. The recession is carried entirely by the
 * planes, which hold no text at all.
 *
 * NO JAVASCRIPT. Not "degrades without it" — there is none. Every element here
 * is visible and correct from the first paint, because the one thing this
 * codebase has already been burned by is content whose correctness depends on
 * an observer firing: a staged reveal on the reasoning trace failed the axe
 * gate with colour-contrast on 7 nodes, and the contrast failure was only the
 * symptom. The animated moments below are all CSS-only, all decorative, all
 * inside aria-hidden subtrees, and none of them exists under
 * prefers-reduced-motion. Not one word on this page is animated at all.
 *
 * ── THE PALETTE RULE, RESOLVED. READ THIS BEFORE CHANGING ANY COLOUR ────────
 *
 * This file previously read "all three planes are teal, and the question is
 * closed". That was the right answer to a question that had been asked
 * imprecisely, and Figma page `139:2` (`Motion — Homepage`, frame `141:2`, "The
 * lighting rig") re-asks it precisely. The resolution is not a relaxation of
 * the palette rule; it is a statement of what the rule governs.
 *
 * THE RULE GOVERNS MARKERS, NOT LIGHT. The test, from `141:2` L01: change an
 * element's hue and ask whether the MEANING changed. If it did, it is a marker
 * and its hue is fixed. If nothing changed, it is light, and the full
 * chromatic range is open. Ambient fields, bloom, surface gradients, rim light
 * and falloff are light. The reasoning trace's orchid cross, the "or dropped"
 * clause on the both-sides rail, the Refused tally row, teal on verified marks
 * and cyan on hover and focus are markers, and none of them is touched here.
 *
 * WHAT WENT WRONG THE FIRST TIME was not that V6 used three hues. It was that
 * it used three FLAT PER-PLANE FILLS: teal, then cyan, then violet, each
 * uniform across its own plane and stopping at its own edge. Three flat colours
 * on three labelled objects read as three CATEGORIES, and a reader could
 * conclude that "Get paid" is violet because violet means something. That is
 * the one conclusion this graphic must never support.
 *
 * TWO THINGS MAKE THE COLOUR SAFE, AND BOTH ARE LOAD-BEARING:
 *
 *   1. HUE FOLLOWS DEPTH CONTINUOUSLY (`141:2` L05). The whole stack sits under
 *      ONE ramp — teal at the source, through cyan, to violet in the falloff —
 *      and each plane takes the third of it that its depth earns. Plane 2
 *      BEGINS on the exact hue plane 1 ENDED on. See --hue-top / --hue-mid /
 *      --hue-bot per row below: `paid`'s --hue-top is character-for-character
 *      `win`'s --hue-bot, and `win`'s --hue-top is `qualify`'s --hue-bot. The
 *      shared rail is painted with the same ramp, so the continuity is visible
 *      as one unbroken line down the axis. IF ANYONE FLATTENS THESE TO ONE FILL
 *      PER PLANE, THE HERO REVERTS TO THE DEFECT ABOVE. That is the first thing
 *      to check on any built result.
 *
 *   2. THE KEY LIGHT HAS A WHITE CORE (`141:2` L04). A marker on this site is a
 *      flat coloured stroke. A lamp is a hot white core with the hue in its
 *      bloom. The travelling light in the rail, and the node it lands on, are
 *      both white-cored, so a reader tells light from label at a glance and the
 *      refusal signal stays unmistakable even with violet back on the page.
 *
 *   And a third, static: the RIM on the two upper edges runs teal to white to
 *   violet and is IDENTICAL on all three planes. A per-plane property that does
 *   not vary per plane is the plainest available proof that hue here is
 *   lighting rather than taxonomy.
 *
 * TEXT IS EXEMPT FROM ALL OF IT. Not one word in this component takes a ramp
 * hue, animates, or changes colour at any point in the cycle. The stage label
 * stays --c-teal and the line stays --c-text, both fixed, both full contrast,
 * from first paint to last frame. Figma's M1 board specifies the stage label
 * lifting --c-text-sub to --c-text as the light lands; that is a text-colour
 * animation on a LOOPING timeline sitting directly beside prose, and the
 * binding rule here is that light goes on strokes, glows and fields behind
 * text and never on text itself. Deliberate divergence, recorded, not an
 * oversight.
 */
import Button from '../ui/Button.astro';

interface Stratum {
  /** Suffix for this row's gradient ids, and the hue selector hook. */
  key: string;
  /** The lifecycle stage. Sentence case; the mono capitals are done in CSS. */
  stage: string;
  /**
   * ONE LINE PER PLANE, DOWN FROM TWO. The rows used to carry a `line` saying
   * what the product does and a `meta` giving the evidence of it doing so, which
   * is 34 words of label on an approved graphic. Owner review, 2026-08-02: the
   * page reads as a research site rather than a product site. The two collapsed
   * into one and the CONCRETE half was kept, because "2 gaps found before you
   * bid" is a product doing something and "Do you clear the gates?" is a
   * question about a category. The geometry is untouched; one text node per row
   * went.
   */
  line: string;
}

/**
 * THE FIGURES HERE ARE PRODUCT CAPABILITY, NOT SOURCED STATISTICS, AND THAT IS
 * DELIBERATE. specs/DESIGN-DECISIONS.md, "No hedging labels on product
 * graphics": a purpose-drawn graphic showing what the product does needs no
 * disclaimer, and the one narrow exception on this site is the 40/30/20% award
 * weightings, which were presented as a real authority's published criteria
 * while naming no authority. "2 gaps found", "82 fit score" and "4 commitments
 * live" claim nothing about an outside body and cite nothing, so there is
 * nothing to source and nothing to hedge.
 *
 * "82 fit score" is fit and rubric coverage. It is NOT a win probability and
 * must never be reworded into one. The competitive register this product is
 * defined against is AutogenAI's "241% increase in success rates"; refusing
 * that claim is the position, so a win-rate number in the hero would cost more
 * than the whole page earns.
 */
const STRATA: Stratum[] = [
  { key: 'qualify', stage: 'Qualify', line: '2 gaps found before you bid' },
  /* "figures traced" was cut from this row, not lost: the lede two elements
     above already says "Every figure traced to evidence you already hold", so
     the row was repeating the hero's own sentence back at it. */
  { key: 'win', stage: 'Win', line: '82 fit score' },
  { key: 'paid', stage: 'Get paid', line: '4 commitments live' },
];

/**
 * PLANE GEOMETRY, TAKEN OFF THE FIGMA FRAME RATHER THAN GUESSED.
 *
 * In `43:3` each plane vector is 500 x 132 at x=600, with centres 140 apart, so
 * consecutive planes clear each other by 8. The viewBox is 504 x 140: that adds
 * 2 either side and 4 top and bottom, which is the room the 1px stroke and its
 * join need at the two acute vertices, and it makes the box height equal to the
 * row pitch so three boxes stacked with no gap reproduce the drawing exactly.
 *
 * These are the graphic's own internal proportions, not a layout width. Nothing
 * else on the page may read them, which is why they are constants here and not
 * tokens; --measure and --gutter remain the only widths this component knows.
 */
const VB = { w: 504, h: 140 };
/** Top, right, bottom, left. The rhombus. */
const FACE = '252,4 502,70 252,136 2,70';
/** Left vertex up to the apex and back down to the right vertex. */
const TOP_EDGES = '2,70 252,4 502,70';
const CX = 252;
const CY = 70;

/**
 * THE BODY OFFSET, IN VIEWBOX UNITS, AND IT IS THE ONE NUMBER IN LEVER 03.
 *
 * specs/ULTRA-PREMIUM-GAP.md §2.2 measured this graphic as line art and was
 * right: three flat polygons at roughly 4% alpha with 1.25px strokes that do
 * not occlude each other, cast onto each other, or have any thickness. The fix
 * is not to redraw the shapes. It is to give each one a body — the same polygon
 * again, opaque, offset downward, painted BEFORE the face — so the face covers
 * its own body's upper half and what is left reads as thickness.
 *
 * WHY 14 AND NOT THE 18 THE DOCUMENT PROPOSES. The frame's own geometry decides
 * it: the vector is 132 tall in a 140 box on a 140 pitch, so consecutive planes
 * clear each other by exactly 8 units. At 18 the body's lower vertex reaches 14
 * units past its own box and 10 units past the next plane's apex, which reads
 * as a collision rather than as depth. At 14 the overlap is small enough that
 * the plane below — painted later, therefore in front, which is the correct
 * order for a stack read downward — cleanly occludes it. That is real occlusion
 * rather than a drawn suggestion of it, and it is what the Figma pair shows.
 */
const BODY_DY = 14;
---

{/*
  `data-spot` is the cursor light's hook (src/scripts/motion.ts). It is written
  as "off" rather than as a bare attribute so the element is selectable before
  any pointer has moved and the stylesheet has one value to test rather than a
  presence check. With no JavaScript it never becomes "on" and the overlay is
  never painted.
*/}
<section class="hero" data-spot="off" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
  {/*
    AMBIENT — the third lamp of the three-point rig (`141:2` L03: one key light
    that moves, one rim that does not, one ambient field behind). Two fields in
    different corners, teal high-left and violet low-right, screen-blended so
    they read as light added to the page rather than as two grey discs painted
    over it. This is the same recipe FinalCta's card already ships, which the
    board names as the reference implementation the other five sections were
    generalised from.

    The vignette is NOT in here. It DARKENS, so it cannot be screen-blended
    with the fields, and it is painted by ::after over the top at normal blend.
    It is a radial in the PAGE colour and never in black.
  */}
  {/*
    `data-field="on"` opts these two into the shared ambient layer declared in
    styles/tokens.css: the always-on --m-drift, and the one-beat m-arrive
    entrance. "on" rather than a bare attribute because the entrance fires at
    LOAD here. Every other section waits for its data-lit to flip; the hero is
    above the fold at every viewport, so it has nothing to wait for.
  */}
  {/*
    THE TWO OVERLAYS GO INSIDE THE WASH, NOT BESIDE IT, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE
    SAFETY ARGUMENT FOR BOTH. The wash is z-index 0 under a z-index 1 content
    layer, so anything mounted in here is behind every word in the hero by
    construction rather than by a value somebody has to keep correct. The
    cursor light can therefore never put light on the surface a word sits on,
    and the grain can never sit over a letterform.

    They are <i> rather than <b> deliberately: the shared ambient layer in
    styles/tokens.css drifts `[data-field] > b` and phases its children by
    :nth-child, so a third <b> here would join the drift and shift the phase
    offsets of the two real fields. A different tag joins neither.
  */}
  <div class="hero__wash" data-field="on" aria-hidden="true">
    <b class="hero__amb hero__amb--1"></b>
    <b class="hero__amb hero__amb--2"></b>
    <b class="hero__amb hero__amb--3"></b>
    <b class="hero__amb hero__amb--4"></b>
    <i class="sv-spot"></i>
    <i class="sv-grain"></i>
  </div>

  <div class="hero__inner">
    {/*
      The eyebrow capsule is the sitewide treatment, owner decision 2026-08-02,
      and the values below are deliberately identical to components/layout/
      Section.astro so the two cannot drift: inline-block, 0.5rem 0.85rem, 1px
      solid var(--c-border), var(--radius-pill), --grad-spectrum clipped to the
      text behind an @supports gate. The hero cannot simply USE Section.astro,
      because Section renders --t-h2 and owns its own head block, and this hero
      is a bespoke approved composition with an h1 and a graphic outside the
      measure rules. Restated, not reinvented. If Section's capsule changes,
      change it here in the same commit.
    */}
    <p class="hero__eyebrow">One engine, the whole contract</p>

    {/*
      GET PAID IS THE GRADIENT SENTENCE, and it is the third one on purpose:
      owner instruction, 2026-08-02. The post-award half is what this product is
      built around and what the site's whole narrative turns on, so it is the
      sentence that gets the light. Qualify and Win stay solid, because three
      gradient sentences would be a background, not an emphasis.

      THE FALLBACK IS THE POINT OF THE SPAN. background-clip: text needs a
      transparent fill, and an inherited -webkit-text-fill-color is exactly what
      caused this site's invisible-text P0. So the span carries a SOLID
      --c-teal in the unlayered base rule, and only inside the @supports gate
      does it become transparent with the gradient painted behind it. A browser
      without background-clip renders solid teal on --c-bg at 7.7:1, which is a
      deliberate emphasis rather than a degradation. Nothing outside this one
      span is touched, so there is no descendant for a text-fill to leak onto.
    */}
    <h1 class="hero__title" id="hero-title">
      Qualify. Win. <span class="hero__paid">Get paid.</span>
    </h1>

    <p class="hero__lede">Every figure traced to evidence you already hold.</p>

    {/*
      TWO REAL CONTROLS, NOT A BUTTON AND A TEXT LINK. Owner review: "'Learn
      more' is not in a button, why?" It is now the shared Button component's
      secondary variant, so the pair is one control system rather than a button
      beside a hand-rolled lookalike. The 54px height, the pill radius, the focus
      ring and the hover all come from ui/Button.astro and are not restated here;
      the ~40 lines of .hero__more CSS that used to duplicate them are gone.

      `label` rather than a bare "Learn more" because a control named only "learn
      more" is meaningless to anyone tabbing a list of links. The label CONTAINS
      the visible text, which WCAG 2.5.3 requires; a label that replaced it would
      break voice control.
    */}
    <div class="hero__actions">
      <Button href="/contact?enquiry=limited-access#contact-form">Request access</Button>
      <Button href="/crowmark" variant="secondary" label="Learn more about how the engine works">
        Learn more
      </Button>
    </div>
  </div>

  {/*
    THE STRATA. A real ordered list of real text, not an image with a
    visually-hidden transcript bolted alongside it. The previous hero needed
    that transcript because its content lived in a depth illusion; here the
    words are the words, in reading order, and only the planes are decorative.
    One source of truth, so the spoken version cannot drift out of step with
    the drawn one.
  */}
  <ol class="strata">
    {
      STRATA.map((s) => (
        <li class="stratum" data-stratum={s.key}>
          <div class="stratum__label">
            <p class="stratum__stage">{s.stage}</p>
            <p class="stratum__line">{s.line}</p>
          </div>

          {/* Draws the label into the plane it belongs to. Pure decoration. */}
          <span class="stratum__wire" aria-hidden="true" />

          <div class="stratum__plane">
            {/*
              The axis. One rail per row, absolutely positioned across the full
              row height, so three rows produce one unbroken line through the
              three nodes however tall a row grows. Drawing it inside the SVG
              instead would break the moment a label wrapped to an extra line.
            */}
            <span class="stratum__axis" aria-hidden="true">
              <span class="stratum__pulse" />
            </span>

            {/* Carries the two-colour glow. Separate from the rail so the
                travelling light never forces the filter to re-evaluate. */}
            <div class="stratum__glow">
              <svg
                class="plane"
                width={VB.w}
                height={VB.h}
                viewBox={`0 0 ${VB.w} ${VB.h}`}
                fill="none"
                aria-hidden="true"
                focusable="false"
              >
                {/*
                  NOT ONE COLOUR LITERAL IN THIS SVG. Every stop takes its
                  colour from a CSS custom property set on the row, resolved in
                  the stylesheet below rather than as an inline attribute, so
                  the palette stays in tokens.css where it belongs and the three
                  instances remain the same drawing three times.

                  THE FACE AND THE EDGE RUN TOP-HUE TO BOTTOM-HUE. That is the
                  continuous ramp: each plane paints its own third of one light,
                  and the next plane starts where this one finished. A single
                  flat hue per plane is the reverted state — see the header note.
                */}
                <defs>
                  <linearGradient
                    id={`hp-face-${s.key}`}
                    x1={CX}
                    y1="4"
                    x2={CX}
                    y2="136"
                    gradientUnits="userSpaceOnUse"
                  >
                    {/*
                      TOP-LIT, 46% DOWN TO 7%. It was 34/14/4, which is a wash
                      so faint that the plane read as its own outline — the
                      "line art" verdict in specs/ULTRA-PREMIUM-GAP.md §2.2.
                      Lever 03 takes the near stop to 34-46% and the far stop
                      to 5-8%, so the surface catches a light falling from
                      above the stack rather than being tinted evenly.

                      THE RAMP IS STILL THREE POINTS ON ONE CONTINUOUS LIGHT.
                      Only the alphas moved; --hue-top / --hue-mid / --hue-bot
                      are untouched, so plane 2 still BEGINS on the hue plane 1
                      ENDED on and the stack is still one ramp in thirds rather
                      than three categories. See the header note.
                    */}
                    <stop class="hp-top" offset="0" stop-opacity="0.46" />
                    <stop class="hp-mid" offset="0.55" stop-opacity="0.20" />
                    <stop class="hp-bot" offset="1" stop-opacity="0.07" />
                  </linearGradient>

                  <linearGradient
                    id={`hp-edge-${s.key}`}
                    x1={CX}
                    y1="4"
                    x2={CX}
                    y2="136"
                    gradientUnits="userSpaceOnUse"
                  >
                    <stop class="hp-top" offset="0" stop-opacity="0.95" />
                    <stop class="hp-bot" offset="1" stop-opacity="0.38" />
                  </linearGradient>

                  {/*
                    THE RIM, AND IT IS IDENTICAL ON ALL THREE PLANES. Teal, to
                    white at the apex, to violet — the same five stops on every
                    row, taking fixed tokens rather than the row's ramp hues.
                    A per-plane decoration that does not vary per plane is the
                    plainest proof available that hue on this graphic is
                    lighting and not category. Do not wire it to --hue-*.
                  */}
                  <linearGradient
                    id={`hp-spec-${s.key}`}
                    x1="2"
                    y1={CY}
                    x2="502"
                    y2={CY}
                    gradientUnits="userSpaceOnUse"
                  >
                    <stop class="hp-rim-out" offset="0" stop-opacity="0" />
                    <stop class="hp-rim-a" offset="0.24" stop-opacity="0.72" />
                    <stop class="hp-rim-core" offset="0.5" stop-opacity="0.95" />
                    <stop class="hp-rim-b" offset="0.76" stop-opacity="0.72" />
                    <stop class="hp-rim-end" offset="1" stop-opacity="0" />
                  </linearGradient>

                  <radialGradient
                    id={`hp-node-${s.key}`}
                    cx={CX}
                    cy={CY}
                    r="20"
                    gradientUnits="userSpaceOnUse"
                  >
                    <stop class="hp-mid" offset="0" stop-opacity="0.7" />
                    <stop class="hp-bot" offset="1" stop-opacity="0" />
                  </radialGradient>
                </defs>

                {/*
                  ── THE BODY, AND IT IS PAINTED FIRST FOR A REASON ──────────
                  Lever 03. The same polygon, offset down by BODY_DY, filled
                  OPAQUE so it occludes whatever is behind it, and drawn before
                  the face so the face covers its own body's upper half. What
                  is left visible is the crescent below the face, which is the
                  plane's thickness.

                  NOTHING IS REDRAWN. It is `points={FACE}` — character for
                  character the approved silhouette — moved by a transform. If
                  this ever becomes a hand-typed second point list, the two can
                  drift and the drawing has been changed rather than lit.

                  IT IS DECORATION INSIDE AN aria-hidden SVG, holds no text, and
                  the whole graphic is still correct with the two lines below
                  deleted.
                */}
                <polygon
                  class="plane__body"
                  points={FACE}
                  transform={`translate(0 ${BODY_DY})`}
                  vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"
                />

                {/*
                  The face. A three-stop ramp rather than a flat fill: a flat
                  fill plus a uniform 1px border is the single strongest
                  PowerPoint tell, and it is the specific verdict the first
                  attempt at this page earned.

                  vector-effect keeps the edge one CSS pixel at every viewport,
                  so the line stays a drawn line instead of thinning to nothing
                  on a phone and fattening on a 4K monitor.
                */}
                <polygon
                  points={FACE}
                  fill={`url(#hp-face-${s.key})`}
                  stroke={`url(#hp-edge-${s.key})`}
                  stroke-width="1"
                  stroke-linejoin="round"
                  vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"
                />

                {/*
                  The specular edge: transparent to accent to transparent along
                  the two upper edges, so the brightest point is the apex. This
                  is what reads as light catching a surface rather than as an
                  outline drawn around a shape.
                */}
                <polyline
                  points={TOP_EDGES}
                  fill="none"
                  stroke={`url(#hp-spec-${s.key})`}
                  stroke-width="1"
                  stroke-linejoin="round"
                  vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"
                />

                {/*
                  The node: a wide bloom under a tight core.

                  BOTH ARE STATIC. They used to be animated as the key light
                  crossed the plane centre — the bloom SPREADING and a third
                  circle flashing WHITE over the core — and both were removed on
                  2026-08-03 under the owner's instruction to stop the animation
                  on the homepage's images. The rest state drawn here was already
                  the floor those animations rose from, so nothing about the
                  drawing changed; it simply stays where it was. A `plane__spark`
                  circle existed solely to be scaled up from zero and is gone
                  rather than left behind at scale(0), where it would have been
                  markup that renders nothing forever.
                */}
                <circle
                  class="plane__bloom"
                  cx={CX}
                  cy={CY}
                  r="20"
                  fill={`url(#hp-node-${s.key})`}
                />
                <circle class="plane__core" cx={CX} cy={CY} r="6" fill="currentColor" />
              </svg>
            </div>
          </div>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ol>

  {/*
    THE CLOSING NOTE. Owner decision, and it is a restoration rather than a new
    line: it shipped on the old hero and was lost in the V6 redesign only
    because the approved frame has no element for it. It goes back.

    IT IS THE STRONGEST SENTENCE ON THE SITE AND IT IS NOT DECORATION. The
    competitive register this product is defined against sells autonomy. This
    says, in eleven words, that the engine does not clear its own gate and that
    a named human approves — which is a substantive claim about how the product
    works, in the same register as the section three below it that watches the
    engine REFUSE a figure. Do not cut it for space and do not soften it into a
    benefit statement.

    IT CLOSES THE HERO RATHER THAN INTERRUPTING IT. Under the strata, so the
    reader meets the product's claim (Qualify, Win, Get paid), then the picture
    of it, and then its limit. Above the strata it would be a caveat before an
    argument. It is the LAST thing in the hero for the same reason it is the
    thing worth remembering.

    NO NEW TYPE SIZE AND NO NEW COLOUR. --t-micro, --font-mono and the tokens
    the eyebrow already uses, so the hero still declares exactly two font sizes
    of small text. It costs the word budget eleven words; the strata rows each
    lost a line in the same pass, so the hero is shorter than it was.
  */}
  <p class="hero__gate">
    <span class="hero__gate-mark" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    The engine cannot clear its own gate. A named person approves.
  </p>
</section>

<style>
  .hero {
    position: relative;
    /* Also the horizontal-overflow guard. The plane glows and the wash both
       paint outside their boxes, and the sitewide gate fails a page whose
       scrollWidth exceeds its clientWidth at 390, 768 or 1440. */
    overflow: hidden;
    padding-block: var(--sec-pad-y);
  }

  /* Sits behind everything the hero renders. z-index rather than a negative
     one, because a negative z-index would put it behind the page background on
     a stacking context this section does not control. */
  .hero__wash {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
    overflow: hidden;
    /* The scroll-driven parallax amplitude for this rig. Declared here rather
       than defaulted so the hero and the six sections below it travel at
       visibly different speeds instead of moving as one sheet — see the
       @supports block in styles/tokens.css. The hero is the tallest field on
       the page and takes the largest throw. */
    --par: 48px;
  }

  /* ══ FOUR FIELDS, READ OFF THE DEPLOYED SITE ═══════════════════════════
   *
   * The owner compared screenshots three times and said the gradient was still
   * wrong. It was, and matching the page wash did not fix it because the wash is
   * not what dominates the hero. This rig is.
   *
   * Sampled at 25 fixed points on both pages at 1440x900, the mismatch was not
   * subtle and it was not a matter of degree:
   *
   *     60,120    live 31,19,62  (violet)   ours  8,15,23  (near black)
   *    360,300    live 26,17,54  (violet)   ours 18,57,59  (teal)
   *   1080,300    live 12,57,68  (cyan)     ours  9,13,21  (near black)
   *
   * LIVE PUTS VIOLET ON THE LEFT AND CYAN ON THE RIGHT. We had teal on the left
   * and violet low right, so the two halves of the hero were the wrong colours,
   * which is why no amount of tuning alpha ever closed it.
   *
   * These are the deployed geometries verbatim: four orbs, blur 90px rather than
   * our 160px, mix-blend-mode: screen, and SOLID colours rather than the
   * alpha-carrying --orb-* tokens, because screen blending over near-black is
   * what produces the light and a pre-multiplied alpha halves it.
   *
   *   1  violet  662px  top -172  left -115   0.55   the source, high left
   *   2  cyan    605px  top   29  left  835   0.42   the answer, high right
   *   3  orchid  547px  top  520  left  490   0.30   low centre, under the stack
   *   4  teal    490px  top  654  left   86   0.34   low left, the falloff
   *
   * Sized in px rather than percentages so a field is the same physical size on
   * a phone and on a 32 inch monitor, which is the only way an ambient rig reads
   * consistently. */
  .hero__amb {
    position: absolute;
    border-radius: 50%;
    filter: blur(90px);
    /* Screen, not normal. These are lights on a near-black page; at normal blend
       they are grey discs sitting on top of it. */
    mix-blend-mode: screen;
  }

  .hero__amb--1 {
    width: 662px; height: 662px;
    top: -172px; left: -115px;
    opacity: 0.55;
    background: var(--orb-1);
  }

  .hero__amb--2 {
    width: 605px; height: 605px;
    top: 29px; left: 835px;
    opacity: 0.42;
    background: var(--orb-2);
  }

  .hero__amb--3 {
    width: 547px; height: 547px;
    top: 520px; left: 490px;
    opacity: 0.3;
    background: var(--orb-3);
  }

  .hero__amb--4 {
    width: 490px; height: 490px;
    top: 654px; left: 86px;
    opacity: 0.34;
    background: var(--orb-4);
  }

  /* The vignette, over the fields at normal blend because it darkens and a
     screen-blended dark layer does nothing. In the PAGE colour rather than
     black, and at absolute px rather than percentages, as above. */
  .hero__wash::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    /* --grad-vignette. The size and centre are this field's own geometry; the
       falloff and strength are the token's, and were 42%/88% here against
       46%/82% on the market section, which is two answers to one question. */
    /* ── OFF, AND MEASURED ────────────────────────────────────────────────
     *
     * This darkened the hero back to the page colour towards its edges. With
     * the ambient rig now built to live's geometry, the vignette is fighting
     * the design rather than supporting it: live carries no such layer over its
     * orbs, and its edge darkness comes from where the orbs are placed.
     *
     * A/B against the deployed page at 20 sampled points, vignette on vs off:
     *
     *     60,120   live 31,19,62    on 12,10,25  d37    off 37,23,68  d6
     *    360,120   live 35,21,69    on 25,16,51  d18    off 37,21,70  d2
     *   1080,120   live  8,26,34    on  8,18,26  d8     off 10,24,34  d2
     *
     * The top of the hero, which is the part a reader meets first and the part
     * the owner screenshotted, moves from badly under-lit to within a few
     * units. It costs a little at the far right edge (1380,120 goes from d3 to
     * d14), so this is a trade rather than a free win, and the mean across all
     * 20 points moves 21.4 to 20.8.
     *
     * Kept as `none` rather than deleted so the geometry above stays readable
     * and one line restores it. */
    --vig: 860px 900px at 50% 32%;
    background: none;
  }

  /* At phone width the two 700px fields are wider than the viewport and a
     160px blur flattens them into one violet-grey sheet, which loses the
     hero's edges rather than lighting them. Scaled with the hero; never
     switched off, because the glow is the design. */
  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .hero__amb {
      filter: blur(96px);
    }
    .hero__amb {
      width: 380px;
      height: 320px;
    }
  }

  /* The shared measure, from tokens. Hardcoding 1160px and clamp(20px,5vw,48px)
     here is how the legacy page ended up with six different max-widths: each
     component knew the number, so no component could be corrected centrally. */
  .hero__inner,
  .hero__gate,
  .strata {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding-inline: var(--gutter);
  }

  /* Centred head block, owner decision 2026-08-02. Scoped to .hero__inner and
     stopping there: it must not reach the strata, whose labels are structured
     content and keep their own alignment. A blanket centring rule is exactly
     what produced the legacy defect of sixteen force-centred element types
     needing 138 per-element opt-outs across 24 pages. */
  .hero__inner {
    text-align: center;
  }

  /* ── The eyebrow capsule ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Identical in every value to .section__eyebrow in layout/Section.astro. See
     the note at the call site above for why it is restated rather than shared.
     The colour is a SECOND signal and never the only one: mono, letter-spaced,
     uppercase, inside a visible outline. Remove all colour and it still reads
     as an eyebrow. WCAG 1.4.1. */
  .hero__eyebrow {
    display: inline-block;
    margin: 0 0 var(--stack-2);
    padding: 0.5rem 0.85rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--c-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    letter-spacing: 0.16em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--c-teal);
  }

  /* background-clip: text needs a transparent fill, and an inherited
     -webkit-text-fill-color is exactly what caused this site's invisible-text
     P0. Gated, scoped to one short label, and axe runs on every route. */
  @supports ((background-clip: text) or (-webkit-background-clip: text)) {
    .hero__eyebrow {
      background: var(--grad-spectrum);
      -webkit-background-clip: text;
      background-clip: text;
      color: transparent;
      -webkit-text-fill-color: transparent;
    }
  }

  .hero__title {
    margin: 0 0 var(--stack-2);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--t-h1);
    font-weight: var(--t-w-h1);
    line-height: 1.04;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    color: var(--c-text);
    /* One line at desktop, and at the widths where it cannot be, two even lines
       rather than four words and an orphan. Unsupported browsers get the normal
       greedy wrap, which is what they have today. */
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  /* ── THE HEADING FADE, WHICH LIVE HAS AND WE DID NOT ────────────────────
   *
   * Owner, 2026-08-03, comparing crowagent.ai against localhost:8095. They were
   * right and I had been checking the wrong thing: I compared the ONE phrase
   * they first mentioned and confirmed it matched, while every heading on the
   * page differed.
   *
   * Read from the deployed site, live paints h1, h2 AND h3 with
   * `linear-gradient(#FFFFFF 0%, rgba(255,255,255,0.6) 100%)` clipped to the
   * text. Ours were flat --c-text. That is a difference on every heading of
   * every page, which is why the page read as "the gradient is not there".
   *
   * --grad-heading ALREADY HELD EXACTLY THAT VALUE and was referenced by
   * nothing. The token was ported across from live and never wired up, so the
   * design system recorded the intent and the pages ignored it. A token nobody
   * references is not a decision, it is a note.
   *
   * IT IS APPLIED WHERE THE COLOUR IS SET, NOT GLOBALLY, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.
   * Astro's scoped component styles are unlayered, so a blanket `h1, h2, h3`
   * rule in a stylesheet would lose to every component that sets `color` on its
   * own heading. Applying it beside the colour it replaces is the only place it
   * reliably wins.
   *
   * THE FALLBACK IS THE SOLID COLOUR ABOVE, kept rather than removed, so a
   * browser without background-clip and forced-colours mode both get readable
   * text. `-webkit-text-fill-color: transparent` INHERITS, which is exactly how
   * this codebase produced invisible text once before, so anything nested that
   * needs its own colour must set it. */
  @supports ((background-clip: text) or (-webkit-background-clip: text)) {
    .hero__title {
      background-image: var(--grad-heading);
      /* ── THE RAMP TILES PER LINE, NOT PER BLOCK ────────────────────────
       *
       * A top-to-bottom fade painted over the whole element means a heading
       * that wraps renders its SECOND line entirely at the dim end. Seen on
       * /pricing: "See the price first." bright, "Sized for SMEs." grey. The
       * deployed site never shows it because its hero is one line.
       *
       * Tiling the background to one line-height gives every line the same
       * ramp, so the effect survives a wrap instead of turning into a fault.
       * `lh` is the correct unit and is recent, so an em fallback matching the
       * declared line-height goes first. */
      background-size: 100% 1.06em;
      background-repeat: repeat;
      -webkit-background-clip: text;
      background-clip: text;
      color: transparent;
      -webkit-text-fill-color: transparent;
    }
  }

  @supports (height: 1lh) {
    .hero__title { background-size: 100% 1lh; }
  }

  @media (forced-colors: active) {
    .hero__title {
      background-image: none;
      color: CanvasText;
      -webkit-text-fill-color: CanvasText;
    }
  }

  /*
   * "GET PAID." IN THE HEADLINE, GRADIENT. Owner instruction, 2026-08-02.
   *
   * THE BASE RULE IS SOLID AND STAYS SOLID unless the gate below matches.
   * --c-teal on --c-bg measures 7.7:1, well over the 3:1 floor large text needs,
   * so a browser with no background-clip support renders a readable emphasised
   * sentence rather than a missing one. This ordering is not stylistic: this
   * site has already shipped a P0 in which an inherited -webkit-text-fill-color
   * made text invisible, and the only safe shape is "readable by default,
   * transparent only where the paint is guaranteed to arrive".
   *
   * WHITE DOWN TO TEAL, NOT --grad-spectrum. The spectrum ramp is the eyebrow's,
   * and running teal to pink across three words inside an otherwise white h1
   * reads as a different typeface rather than as emphasis. This is
   * --grad-heading's idea, a display face resolving downward, ending on the
   * brand's verified colour instead of on transparency: the sentence starts the
   * same white as the two beside it and arrives somewhere. Both ends are token
   * colours and both clear 7:1 on --c-bg, so no part of the glyph is ever the
   * low-contrast end of a ramp. No hex is typed here; the one mixed stop is
   * composed from two tokens.
   *
   * SCOPED TO THIS SPAN AND NOTHING BELOW IT. The span has no element children,
   * so there is no descendant for a text-fill to leak onto, which is the exact
   * mechanism of the original defect.
   */
  .hero__paid {
    color: var(--c-teal);
  }

  @supports ((background-clip: text) or (-webkit-background-clip: text)) {
    .hero__paid {
      /* ── THE SAME SPECTRUM AS THE SECTION CAPSULES ────────────────────────
       *
       * Owner, 2026-08-03: "make the hero Get paid. text gradient multicolour
       * like the section capsules have."
       *
       * This was a private two-stop ramp, white down to a teal-cyan mix. Two
       * things were wrong with it. It was a single-hue ramp where the capsules
       * run the full spectrum, so the page's most prominent gradient did not
       * match the gradient it appears beside twelve times. And it was written
       * here rather than taken from a token, which is the exact duplication
       * rule 7 of the design-system gate exists to stop.
       *
       * CORRECTED an hour later, and the correction is the point. My first fix
       * used --grad-spectrum, the CAPSULE gradient. The live site does not paint
       * this phrase with the capsule gradient: read from the running legacy
       * build, the element whose text is "Get paid." carries three stops at
       * 100deg, teal through cyan to violet, with no pink. --grad-hero-text now
       * holds exactly that. See its note in tokens.css, which also records what
       * the phrase looked like before 31 July. */
      background-image: var(--grad-hero-text);
      -webkit-background-clip: text;
      background-clip: text;
      color: transparent;
      -webkit-text-fill-color: transparent;
    }
  }

  /* Windows high-contrast throws user styling away, and a transparent fill
     there is an invisible sentence. Forced back to system text: an accessibility
     override, which tokens.css names as the one legitimate exception to the
     no-escalation rule. */
  @media (forced-colors: active) {
    .hero__paid {
      background-image: none;
      color: CanvasText;
      -webkit-text-fill-color: CanvasText;
    }
  }

  .hero__lede {
    margin: 0 auto var(--stack-2);
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-lede);
    line-height: 1.65;
    /* Matches .section__standfirst. Centred text needs a centred measure or the
       line sits left inside a centred block. */
    max-width: 62ch;
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
  }

  .hero__actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 0.75rem;
  }

  /* NOTHING STYLES THE ACTIONS BUT THE FLEX ROW ABOVE. "Learn more" was a
     hand-rolled lookalike of a control here, ~40 lines restating --btn-h, the
     pill radius, the font size and weight, the hover and the focus ring. It is
     ui/Button.astro's secondary variant now, so those forty lines are deleted
     rather than commented out: a second definition of a button is how the nine
     hand-typed copies this codebase already removed got written in the first
     place. Cyan survives in this component only as the interactive colour on
     that component's own hover, which is where the palette rule puts it. */

  /* ── The closing note ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Centred with the head block above, sitting on the same measure so it lines
     up with the copy rather than with the graphic's left bias. --t-micro and
     --font-mono, borrowed from the eyebrow, so the hero introduces no new
     size. --c-text-sub measures 14.48:1 on --c-bg; it is quiet, not dim, and
     nothing here animates. */
  .hero__gate {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    margin: var(--sec-gap) auto 0;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    line-height: 1.6;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
    text-align: center;
    text-wrap: balance;
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
  }

  /* A single teal stop before the line. Teal is the verified colour and a
     named approver is the site's verification claim, so the mark is doing the
     same job it does everywhere else. It is decoration and aria-hidden: remove
     all colour and the sentence is unchanged, which is WCAG 1.4.1. */
  .hero__gate-mark {
    flex: none;
    width: 6px;
    height: 6px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--c-teal);
    box-shadow: 0 0 8px var(--c-teal-glow);
  }

  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    /* At 350px of content box the dot and the sentence fight for the same line
       and the wrap lands badly. The mark goes; it carries nothing. */
    .hero__gate-mark { display: none; }
  }

  /* ── The strata ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────*/

  .strata {
    margin: var(--sec-pad-y) auto 0;
    /* padding-block only. A blanket `padding: 0` would also cancel the
       padding-inline set by the shared measure rule above, which would start
       the graphic a full gutter to the left of the copy it belongs to. The
       <ol> default padding-inline-start is already overridden there, so there
       is nothing left to zero on the inline axis. */
    padding-block: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  /* ── ONE RAMP, IN THIRDS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
   *
   * The stack sits under a single light: TEAL at the source, through CYAN, to
   * VIOLET in the falloff. Each row takes the third of that ramp its depth
   * earns, sampled at three points so the row's own face, edge and node all
   * resolve from the same three values.
   *
   * READ THE THREE BLOCKS BELOW VERTICALLY, NOT HORIZONTALLY. The continuity
   * is the entire design and it is visible in the source:
   *
   *     qualify  top  --c-teal
   *              mid  33% cyan
   *              bot  67% cyan   ─┐
   *     win      top  67% cyan   ─┘  the same value, written the same way
   *              mid  --c-cyan
   *              bot  33% violet ─┐
   *     paid     top  33% violet ─┘  the same value, written the same way
   *              mid  67% violet
   *              bot  --c-violet
   *
   * Plane 2 BEGINS on the hue plane 1 ENDED on, and plane 3 on plane 2's. Three
   * flat per-plane fills would read as three categories and would put the hero
   * straight back into the defect the header note describes. If you are about
   * to replace one of these blocks with a single --hue, stop and read it.
   *
   * NOTHING THAT HOLDS TEXT READS THESE. They reach the face, the edge, the
   * node, the rail, the wire and the glow, all of which are aria-hidden
   * decoration. The stage label and the line take fixed tokens, below.
   *
   * --lift IS STILL THE ELEVATION LADDER and still carries the sequence: it
   * drives brightness and glow spread, so "Get paid" sits forward of "Win"
   * which sits forward of "Qualify". Hue carries depth; lift carries elevation;
   * they say the same thing twice, which is what makes it read as one object.
   */
  .stratum {
    /* Defensive defaults. Every row sets its own three; a row that somehow did
       not would render as flat teal rather than disappear. */
    --hue-top: var(--c-teal);
    --hue-mid: var(--c-teal);
    --hue-bot: var(--c-teal);
    --lift: 0.86;
  }

  .stratum[data-stratum='qualify'] {
    --hue-top: var(--c-teal);
    --hue-mid: color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-cyan) 33%, var(--c-teal));
    --hue-bot: color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-cyan) 67%, var(--c-teal));
    /* THE FLOOR WAS 0.60/0.75/0.90 AND IS NOW 0.86/0.93/1.00. Lever 03: at a
       0.6 floor the top plane is a bit over half lit and reads as half dead,
       which is depth carried by dimming rather than by geometry. The LADDER is
       preserved exactly — "Get paid" still sits forward of "Win" which sits
       forward of "Qualify" — it is just a ladder inside the top sixth of the
       range instead of the top half. The glow radii still scale with it, so
       elevation is still said twice. */
    --lift: 0.86;
  }

  .stratum[data-stratum='win'] {
    --hue-top: color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-cyan) 67%, var(--c-teal));
    --hue-mid: var(--c-cyan);
    --hue-bot: color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-violet) 33%, var(--c-cyan));
    --lift: 0.93;
  }

  .stratum[data-stratum='paid'] {
    --hue-top: color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-violet) 33%, var(--c-cyan));
    --hue-mid: color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-violet) 66%, var(--c-cyan));
    --hue-bot: var(--c-violet);
    --lift: 1;
  }

  /* ── The stops, wired to the ramp ────────────────────────────────────────
     stop-color is a CSS property, so the gradient stops read the row's custom
     properties through the cascade exactly like any other declaration. That is
     what keeps every colour in this component in tokens.css. */
  .hp-top { stop-color: var(--hue-top); }
  .hp-mid { stop-color: var(--hue-mid); }
  .hp-bot { stop-color: var(--hue-bot); }

  /* The rim, fixed and identical on all three planes. See the note at the
     gradient itself: it must not be wired to --hue-*. */
  /* The two outer stops are fully transparent, so their colour only matters for
     what the ramp INTERPOLATES TOWARDS as it fades. Each therefore matches the
     lit stop beside it: teal at the left end, violet at the right. A single
     shared class here would fade the violet half back through teal. */
  .hp-rim-out { stop-color: var(--c-teal); }
  .hp-rim-a { stop-color: var(--c-teal); }
  .hp-rim-core { stop-color: var(--c-text); }
  .hp-rim-b { stop-color: var(--c-violet); }
  .hp-rim-end { stop-color: var(--c-violet); }

  .stratum {
    display: grid;
    /* THE PROPORTIONS ARE THE APPROVED FRAME'S at the 1160 measure — label 320,
       wire 140, plane 500 — as fr rather than px, so the graphic scales as one
       drawing instead of three hand-tuned widths.

       THE AIR IS NOW SYMMETRIC. Owner, 2026-08-03: "the hero section 3 horizon
       must be centrally aligned, currently it is left aligned in the page."

       This was 320 / 140 / 500 and then 200fr of TRAILING air, and the comment
       here defended that as "a deliberate left bias". Measured, the bias was
       real and larger than it reads on paper: content occupied 188..1069 in a
       1064px row, so the whole three-row assembly sat 91.7px left of the page
       centre — exactly half the empty fourth track — while the h1, the
       standfirst and the button pair above it were all centred on 720. The
       drawing disagreed with everything it sits under.

       Splitting the air 100/100 keeps every internal proportion the approved
       frame specified and moves nothing but the assembly's position in the row.
       The label column, the wire run and the plane all keep their exact
       relative widths; what changes is that the composition now shares the page
       centre line with the type above it. */
    grid-template-columns:
      minmax(0, 100fr)
      minmax(0, 320fr)
      minmax(0, 140fr)
      minmax(0, 500fr)
      minmax(0, 100fr);
    /* Stretch, not center. The plane cell has to be the full height of the row
       or the rail inside it cannot span the row, and the rail spanning the row
       is what makes three separate elements read as one continuous axis. */
    align-items: stretch;
  }

  /* PLACED EXPLICITLY, because the leading air track is column 1. Without this
     the three children auto-flow into columns 1, 2 and 3 — the label would sit
     in the air and the plane would land where the wire belongs, which is a
     worse composition than the left bias this change exists to remove. */
  .stratum__label {
    grid-column: 2;
    align-self: center;
  }

  .stratum__wire { grid-column: 3; }
  .stratum__plane { grid-column: 4; }

  /* FIXED TEAL ON ALL THREE ROWS, and it does not animate. This is the one
     place the ramp deliberately does NOT reach: it is text, and a violet stage
     label would be exactly the marker read the whole design is arranged to
     prevent. See the header note under "TEXT IS EXEMPT FROM ALL OF IT". */
  .stratum__stage {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.16em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--c-teal);
  }

  /* The row's only sentence now, so it takes the display face and the full
     --c-text. Nothing in this component may carry depth by dimming or blurring a
     word: the recession is the planes' job and they hold no text at all. */
  .stratum__line {
    margin: var(--stack-1) 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--t-h4);
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1.25;
    color: var(--c-text);
  }

  .stratum__wire {
    align-self: center;
    height: 1px;
    /* Percentages of its own track, so the inset stays proportional as the
       whole graphic scales. */
    margin-inline: 8% 12%;
    /* --grad-fade, running the other way: 270deg puts the colour at the left
       and the fade at the right. 70% rather than the token's 55% default is
       kept deliberately — this is a drawn wire behind a diagram, not the end of
       a rail, and it has no lit dot at its bright end to carry it. */
    --fade-angle: 270deg;
    --fade-hue: var(--hue-mid);
    --fade-strength: 70%;
    background: var(--grad-fade);
  }

  .stratum__plane {
    position: relative;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    /* The node core inherits this. Everything else in the SVG resolves from
       --hue-top/mid/bot through the stop classes above. Set here rather than on
       .stratum so it can never leak into the label text. */
    color: var(--hue-mid);
  }

  /*
   * THE RAIL, AND IT IS WHERE THE CONTINUITY IS VISIBLE. Absolute across the
   * full row height so consecutive rows abut with no seam, and left:50% is
   * exact because the plane cell and the drawing are the same width and the
   * node sits at 252 of 504.
   *
   * It is painted with the row's OWN third of the ramp, top hue to bottom hue.
   * Because each row's --hue-top is the row above's --hue-bot, the three
   * separate elements draw one unbroken teal-to-cyan-to-violet line down the
   * whole graphic with no visible join. That line is the proof the reader can
   * see: three flat rails would show three butt joints, and this one does not.
   */
  .stratum__axis {
    position: absolute;
    inset-block: 0;
    left: 50%;
    width: 2px;
    margin-left: -1px;
    overflow: hidden;
    /* --grad-ramp. The two ends are the row's own third of the brand ramp,
       which is what makes three separate elements draw one unbroken line. */
    --ramp-from: color-mix(in srgb, var(--hue-top) 55%, transparent);
    --ramp-to: color-mix(in srgb, var(--hue-bot) 55%, transparent);
      /* Written here, not var(--grad-ramp). A custom property's var()s are
         substituted where it is DECLARED, so a knob token on :root bakes in its
         fallbacks and inherits finished. Measured: --grad-ramp computes to
         `linear-gradient(180deg, #161B2F, #0E1220 100%)` on every element,
         whatever knobs it sets. It hid every blog hero photo behind an opaque
         rectangle before this was found. Colours still come from tokens. */
    background: linear-gradient(
      180deg,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--hue-top) 55%, transparent),
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--hue-bot) 55%, transparent) 100%
    );
  }

  /* THE RAIL STOPS AT THE FIRST AND LAST NODES, with an 18px overshoot that
     fades out. The overshoot is the whole blueprint effect: a rail that stops
     dead on the node reads as a join, a rail that runs 18px past it and fades
     reads as a longer line the drawing has cropped. 50% of the cell is exactly
     the node, because the drawing is centred in the cell and the node is
     centred in the drawing, whatever height the row settles at.

     The fade is a MASK rather than a second background, so the ramp above is
     the only thing declaring colour and the two cannot drift apart. */
  .stratum:first-child .stratum__axis {
    top: calc(50% - 18px);
    -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(180deg, transparent, rgb(0 0 0) 18px);
    mask-image: linear-gradient(180deg, transparent, rgb(0 0 0) 18px);
  }

  .stratum:last-child .stratum__axis {
    bottom: calc(50% - 18px);
    -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(0deg, transparent, rgb(0 0 0) 18px);
    mask-image: linear-gradient(0deg, transparent, rgb(0 0 0) 18px);
  }

  /* TWO-COLOUR GLOW: a tight 12px core in teal under a wide 30px halo in
     teal-dark. Applied to this wrapper rather than to the SVG so the travelling
     light inside the rail is outside the filtered subtree and cannot force the
     blur to be recomputed on every frame.

     BOTH RADII AND THE WHOLE WRAPPER SCALE WITH --lift. That is the elevation
     half of the ladder: a plane that sits forward is brighter AND throws a
     wider glow, which is the same thing light does. The opacity here is safe
     and is not the depth-by-degraded-text failure this component was built to
     avoid: this wrapper contains the aria-hidden SVG and nothing else, and every
     word in the hero sits outside it at a full-contrast token colour. */
  /*
   * THREE PARTS, NOT TWO, AND THE THIRD IS THE ONE THAT MAKES IT AN OBJECT.
   * Lever 03 in specs/ULTRA-PREMIUM-GAP.md: a 12px hue core, a 40px violet
   * halo, and a 34px BLACK CAST at y+22. The first two are light coming off the
   * plane and were already here. The third is the plane's shadow falling onto
   * whatever is behind it, which is the only one of the three that says the
   * plane is in front of something rather than glowing in a void.
   *
   * THE HALO IS VIOLET ON ALL THREE PLANES, deliberately, and for the identical
   * reason the rim is teal-white-violet on all three: a per-plane decoration
   * that does not vary per plane is the plainest available proof that hue here
   * is lighting rather than taxonomy. It was the row's own --hue-bot, which
   * varied, and a varying halo is one more thing a reader could mistake for a
   * category.
   *
   * The cast is composed from --c-floor rather than typed as a black, so it is
   * the page's own deepest step and recolours with the ladder.
   */
  .stratum__glow {
    width: 100%;
    opacity: var(--lift);
    filter: drop-shadow(
        0 0 calc(12px * var(--lift)) color-mix(in srgb, var(--hue-mid) 55%, transparent)
      )
      drop-shadow(0 0 calc(40px * var(--lift)) color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-violet) 34%, transparent))
      drop-shadow(0 22px 34px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-floor) 85%, transparent));
  }

  /* width/height attributes are on the element as well, so the drawing has an
     intrinsic size and a reserved aspect ratio even before this rule applies.
     No layout shift, and no dependence on viewBox ratio inference. */
  .plane {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    /*
     * THE BODY IS DRAWN OUTSIDE THE VIEWBOX AND HAS TO BE ALLOWED OUT.
     * BODY_DY moves the clone 14 units down and the silhouette already reaches
     * y=136 of a 140 box, so the lower vertex lands 10 units past the bottom
     * edge. An SVG root clips to its viewport by default, which would cut the
     * body off in a straight line exactly where it is supposed to be a point.
     *
     * It is safe on the block axis and it is only the block axis: the drawing
     * is width-constrained by its grid track, so nothing here can widen the
     * page, and .hero already sets overflow: hidden as the horizontal guard.
     */
    overflow: visible;
  }

  /*
   * THE BODY. Opaque, so it occludes; two steps of the ladder, so it is a
   * surface rather than a shadow; and a stroke at the row's own falloff hue, so
   * the thickness catches the same light the face does.
   *
   * It takes --hue-bot because a body is the underside, and the underside is
   * the far end of the ramp. It never takes --hue-top: a body lit like the face
   * above it reads as a second face, which is the drawing this file must not
   * become.
   */
  .plane__body {
    fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--hue-bot) 13%, var(--c-floor));
    stroke: color-mix(in srgb, var(--hue-bot) 34%, transparent);
    stroke-width: 1;
    stroke-linejoin: round;
  }

  /* ══ THE KEY LIGHT ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   *
   * Figma `142:2`, M1 — "One light falls through three planes, and the hue
   * falls with it". TRIGGER page load, CSS only, no observer.
   * CADENCE 6 beats per rail, 2 beats apart, on the shared --m-cycle.
   * PLAYS: LOOPS, and now so does every other section.
   *
   * WHAT CHANGED, AND WHY THE NUMBERS MOVED AT ALL. This section used to be the
   * only one on the page that looped, at 3,900ms per rail with a 1,300ms
   * stagger — its own numbers, agreeing with nothing else. The owner has since
   * asked for continuous play across the whole homepage and for the sections to
   * read as one system, so the hero is now expressed in the same units as the
   * other six: --m-beat x 6 of travel and --m-beat x 2 of stagger, which is
   * 3,600ms and 1,200ms. See the motion grammar block in styles/tokens.css.
   *
   * The RELATIONSHIP is untouched, and it is the part that matters: the stagger
   * is still exactly a third of the pass, so the light still leaves one plane as
   * it reaches the next and the three rails still read as one descent rather
   * than as three loops. What is new is the rest. The three passes occupy
   * 0-6,000ms of the 12s cycle and the rail is dark for the other six seconds,
   * which is what makes it read as a lamp crossing rather than as a conveyor.
   *
   * ONE THING MOVES, AND IT USED TO BE FOUR:
   *   pulse    the light travelling down the rail            (translate)
   *
   * The other three — spark, bloom and surface — landed AT the node and lifted
   * the plane's own ramp, so all three changed the drawing rather than crossing
   * it. The owner asked on 2026-08-03 for the animation on the homepage's images
   * to stop, and those three are what that meant here. The travelling light
   * stays, because it is the same light six other sections paint with
   * --grad-beam and the owner has separately asked for that to run continuously
   * and in symmetry across the page.
   *
   * WHAT IS LOST, STATED PLAINLY: the landing. The light now crosses the plane
   * centre and nothing happens there. The pass, the stagger, the rest and the
   * relationship between the three rails are all untouched, so the section still
   * reads as one descent — it simply no longer arrives at anything. If the owner
   * wants an arrival back, the honest way to give it is on the RAIL rather than
   * on the drawing, which is a design decision and not a restore.
   *
   * EVERY ONE OF THEM IS INSIDE AN aria-hidden SUBTREE, is transform or
   * opacity only so it stays on the compositor, and rises from a rest state
   * that is already correct. Nothing dims below its rest value at any point,
   * which is binding rule 02. If none of this runs, the hero is the finished
   * drawing with a static ramp on it, and nothing is missing — the rule this
   * codebase holds after sv-reveal left 7 of 9 homepage sections permanently
   * invisible when its observer did not fire.
   *
   * NO OBSERVER AND NO SCRIPT. `data-lit` and src/scripts/motion.ts drive the
   * other five sections; the hero is above the fold on every viewport, so
   * asking an IntersectionObserver whether it is visible is a question with a
   * known answer and a failure mode.
   */
  .stratum__pulse {
    display: none;
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    /* THE TRAVELLING LIGHT. White core, hue bloom either side of it — `141:2`
       L04, and the single clearest tell separating a lamp from a marker on
       this site. A marker is a flat coloured stroke; nothing else on this page
       has a hot white centre. The band is ~20% of the rail, which is roughly
       the 26px the board specifies at the row heights this graphic settles at. */
    .stratum__pulse {
      display: block;
      position: absolute;
      inset-inline: 0;
      /* Full rail height, with the visible band drawn inside it, so a
         translateY of 100% is exactly one rail and the travel is predictable
         at any row height. */
      height: 100%;
      /* --grad-beam, running down the rail. The band is 20% of the rail, which
         is roughly the 26px the board specifies at the row heights this
         graphic settles at. Bloom was 65% here and 70% in four other sections;
         the token holds 70% and this is one of the five opinions it ends. */
      --beam-angle: 180deg;
      --beam-hue: var(--hue-mid);
      --beam-w: 10%;
      background: var(--grad-beam);
      animation: strata-fall var(--m-cycle) var(--m-ease-travel) infinite;
    }

    /*
     * ── THREE ANIMATIONS USED TO LIVE HERE AND THEY ARE GONE ─────────────────
     *
     * Owner, 2026-08-03: "stop all the animations on the home page images".
     * These three were the only ones on this page that moved a DRAWING:
     *
     *   strata-surface  filter: brightness(1 -> 1.16) on the <svg class="plane">
     *                   itself, so the whole picture pulsed
     *   strata-bloom    transform: scale(1 -> 1.4) on <circle class="plane__bloom">
     *   strata-spark    transform: scale(0 -> 0.62) on <circle class="plane__spark">
     *
     * THE FALLING RAIL LIGHT BELOW STAYS, and the distinction is the rule the
     * whole sweep was applied on: a light that TRAVELS ACROSS a figure is the
     * site's shared motion grammar, painted with --grad-beam in six other
     * sections, and the owner has separately asked for that motion to be
     * continuous and symmetric across the page. .stratum__pulse is that light
     * here. A light that blooms, sparks or brightens IN PLACE is not travelling,
     * it is the picture changing, and that is what the owner asked to stop.
     *
     * SO THE ROW IS STILL A FOUR-THINGS-ARE-ONE-EVENT RIG, with one thing in it
     * rather than four, and the stagger below is unchanged: two beats apart is
     * still what makes three rails read as one descent.
     */

    /* TWO BEATS APART, which is one third of the six-beat pass, so the light
       leaves one plane as it reaches the next and the three rails read as one
       descent rather than as three loops.

       A delay on an INFINITE animation is a permanent phase offset rather than
       a one-off wait, so the stagger survives every repeat of the cycle. */
    .stratum:nth-child(2) .stratum__pulse {
      animation-delay: calc(var(--m-beat) * 2);
    }

    .stratum:nth-child(3) .stratum__pulse {
      animation-delay: calc(var(--m-beat) * 4);
    }
  }

  /* ── READING THE PERCENTAGES IN THIS BLOCK ────────────────────────────────
     Every keyframe below runs over --m-cycle, not over the pass, because that
     is how a light gets to cross AND THEN REST inside one looping animation.
     The pass is six beats and --m-cycle is twenty, so the whole travel happens
     between 0% and 30% and every keyframe after that is the rest state held
     flat to 100%. One beat is exactly 5%, which is the reason --m-cycle is
     defined as twenty beats rather than as a round number of seconds: nothing
     in here needs a calculator to check. */
  @keyframes strata-fall {
    0% {
      transform: translateY(-100%);
      opacity: 0;
    }
    5% {
      opacity: 1;
    }
    25% {
      opacity: 1;
    }
    30% {
      transform: translateY(100%);
      opacity: 0;
    }
    /* The rest. Off the bottom of the rail and invisible, which is the same
       state the element holds when nothing runs at all. */
    100% {
      transform: translateY(100%);
      opacity: 0;
    }
  }

  /* strata-surface, strata-bloom and strata-spark were declared here. All three
     animated the drawing itself and were removed on 2026-08-03; see the note in
     the no-preference block above. The keyframes go with them rather than being
     left in place "in case" — an unreferenced @keyframes block is the thing a
     later section copies without reading why it stopped being used. */

  /* ══ THE ROW ANSWERS A CURSOR ═════════════════════════════════════════════
   *
   * specs/ULTRA-PREMIUM-GAP.md §2.6: 2.7% of this page's elements carry a CSS
   * transition against 16.2% on the dark comparator, and four of seven sections
   * — this one included — had ZERO :hover rules. "Nothing on this page answers
   * a cursor. That is what dead is."
   *
   * WHAT THE ROW DOES WHEN THE POINTER IS ON IT: its plane comes fully up, its
   * body lights, its node core throws a wider shadow, its wire reaches further
   * towards the drawing, its rail brightens, and a soft glow appears behind the
   * two lines of text. Six things, one event, and the light is what moves.
   *
   * FOUR RULES ARE OBEYED HERE AND EVERY ONE OF THEM CONSTRAINED THE DESIGN.
   *
   *   The row holds text, so NOTHING TRANSLATES. styles/surfaces.css restricts
   *   the card lift to elements that are actually interactive, on the grounds
   *   that lifting a paragraph advertises an affordance that is not there and
   *   moves the line somebody is reading. A stratum row is a label beside a
   *   drawing; it navigates nowhere. So the row never moves.
   *
   *   NO LIGHT ON THE SURFACE A WORD SITS ON. There is no background change
   *   anywhere in this block. The two text rules take a text-shadow, which is a
   *   glow BEHIND the glyph and cannot alter what the glyph is measured
   *   against — the same construction the both-sides refusal already ships and
   *   the same reason it was chosen there.
   *
   *   NOT ONE COLOUR CHANGES MEANING. The stage label stays --c-teal and the
   *   line stays --c-text at every frame. What changes is how much light is
   *   around them.
   *
   *   THE HOVER REACHES NOTHING A READER NEEDS. Every property below is on an
   *   aria-hidden shape or is a glow. Cover the pointer and the row is complete.
   *
   * (hover: hover) so a tap on a phone cannot latch a row into this state and
   * leave it there, and the transforms — there are none here — would be removed
   * under reduced motion by styles/surfaces.css in any case.
   */
  .stratum__glow,
  .plane__body,
  .plane__core,
  .stratum__wire,
  .stratum__axis,
  .stratum__line,
  .stratum__stage {
    transition:
      opacity var(--dur) var(--ease),
      filter var(--dur) var(--ease),
      fill var(--dur) var(--ease),
      stroke var(--dur) var(--ease),
      margin var(--dur) var(--ease),
      text-shadow var(--dur) var(--ease);
  }

  @media (hover: hover) {
    /* The plane comes to full light, and its cast deepens rather than
       widening: a plane nearer the light throws a harder shadow, not a bigger
       glow. Same three-part construction, one step up. */
    .stratum:hover .stratum__glow {
      opacity: 1;
      filter: drop-shadow(0 0 16px color-mix(in srgb, var(--hue-mid) 72%, transparent))
        drop-shadow(0 0 54px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-violet) 46%, transparent))
        drop-shadow(0 26px 38px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-floor) 92%, transparent));
    }

    .stratum:hover .plane__body {
      fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--hue-bot) 22%, var(--c-floor));
      stroke: color-mix(in srgb, var(--hue-bot) 55%, transparent);
    }

    .stratum:hover .plane__core {
      filter: drop-shadow(0 0 10px color-mix(in srgb, var(--hue-mid) 85%, transparent));
    }

    /* Reaches further towards the plane it points at. It is alone in its own
       grid track, so the inline margin is the only thing this can move and no
       text is laid out again. */
    .stratum:hover .stratum__wire {
      margin-inline: 4% 4%;
      filter: brightness(1.6);
    }

    .stratum:hover .stratum__axis { filter: brightness(1.5); }

    .stratum:hover .stratum__line {
      text-shadow: 0 0 22px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-text) 34%, transparent);
    }

    .stratum:hover .stratum__stage {
      text-shadow: 0 0 16px var(--c-teal-glow);
    }
  }

  /* ── Phone ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     At 390 the content box is 350px. A 320/140/500/200 split of that gives the
     label 96px and the plane 151px, which wraps every line to four words and
     renders a 40px tall diamond. The split is what fails, so it is dropped
     rather than squeezed: label above plane, full width, in reading order.

     The wire and the rail go with it. Both exist to join a left column to a
     right one, and once the rows are stacked the rail would run straight
     through the label text it is supposed to point at. Both are decorative and
     aria-hidden, so removing them removes nothing but decoration. */
  @media (max-width: 767px) {
    .stratum {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      /* ── THE EXPLICIT PLACEMENT HAS TO BE UNDONE HERE ──────────────────
       *
       * Desktop places the three children in columns 2, 3 and 4 because column
       * 1 is the leading air track that centres the composition. Those
       * declarations do not stop applying when the template collapses to one
       * column: the browser creates IMPLICIT tracks to satisfy them.
       *
       * Measured at 390 before this: `grid-template-columns` computed to
       * `0px 206.922px 0px 143.078px` — four tracks, not one — so the label sat
       * in a 207px column on the left and the plane in a 143px column on the
       * right, at a different size on every row. The stack read as ragged
       * rather than as three equal horizons.
       *
       * No gate saw it. There was no overflow, no contrast failure and no
       * undersized target; it was simply wrong, and only a screenshot at 390
       * showed it. */
      grid-template-rows: auto;
      row-gap: var(--stack-1);
      padding-block: var(--stack-2);
    }

    .stratum__label,
    .stratum__wire,
    .stratum__plane {
      grid-column: auto;
    }

    .stratum__wire,
    .stratum__axis {
      display: none;
    }
  }
</style>
